#
# Wireless LAN and Bluetooth
#
# The Steam Deck LCD is Realtek RTL8822CE; MediaTek MT7921/MT7922 are the
# common M.2 cards. (The Deck OLED is Qualcomm QCA2066, ath11k, which is
# not here.)
#
# Firmware. The rule was that this repository carries no linux-firmware
# blobs, and without them each driver here loads and its interface never
# appears. The rule now: no blob is in git, and a profile names every file
# its drivers ask for, with the SHA-256 of that file at the linux-firmware
# commit kernel/firmware/apply pins. The build fetches each one, refuses a
# digest that differs, and puts it in the initramfs as /lib/firmware/NAME.zst
# beside the licence texts and the WHENCE sections that cover it. The
# drivers find them at probe time; there is no user helper.
#
#   rtw88       rtw8822c_fw.bin and rtw8822c_wow_fw.bin: an 8822C refuses to
#               power on without either
#   rtl_bt      rtl8822cu_fw.bin; rtl8822cu_config.bin is upstream's link
#               to rtl8761bu_config.bin, carried so no boot logs its absence
#   mt7921e     the MT7961 (MT7921) and MT7922 patch and RAM code
#   btmtk       the MT7961 and MT7922 bluetooth RAM code
#   btrtl       the common USB adapters' and combo cards' Bluetooth: RTL8761BU
#               (most USB sticks; its config is the one rtl8822cu_config.bin
#               is taken from), RTL8852BU and RTL8852CU (Wi-Fi 6 and 6E
#               combos), RTL8821CU and RTL8822BU; the 8852 parts ship no
#               config file and need none
#   btintel     Intel's Bluetooth, which boots from its bootloader and does
#               nothing until it is given these: AX200 (ibt-20-1-3), AX201 and
#               9560 (ibt-19-0-4), AX210 (ibt-0041-0041), AX211 (ibt-0040-0041)
#               and BE200 (ibt-0291-0291), each .sfi with its .ddc. The other
#               names upstream links to these are not carried (each link is a
#               whole copy here); about 2.7 MB of the image and of its RAM
#
# The regulatory database. cfg80211 asks for regulatory.db as it starts, and
# with the signature required (below) for regulatory.db.p7s as well; without
# them every boot logs "failed to load regulatory.db" and every radio keeps
# the world domain. They are wireless-regdb's, not linux-firmware's, so the
# lines say regdb: kernel/firmware/apply fetches them from the one release it
# pins and checks each digest, as it does the files above.
